MAP: ways of knowing together
=============================

What this is
------------
A working map of how participants come to know things together, and of the kinds of
coordination that rest on that knowing. It starts from two sources kept in source\:

  ATLAS.md          the Atlas of Coordination
  EPISTEMOLOGY.md   a reading of the Atlas as an epistemology: five commitments, four axes,
                    nine types, a shadow typology, five tensions

The map takes the reading's structure and adds four things: traditions outside the Atlas that
already practise each type, the smallest practice each type implies, how agents might carry it
out, and a closer look at the shadows.

Neither source is a standard this map must meet. Both are claims to try. Where a run shows a
part of either one failing, the finding goes in CRITIQUE, and the map changes. Section numbers
(§) refer to the Atlas.

Anyone taking part may revise this map: write the new text, your name, and why. Places where
the meaning is still open are marked with "…", as the Atlas marks its own.


The commitments underneath (from the reading)
---------------------------------------------
  1. Knowing depends on disclosure. Intention, capacity, boundaries and harm are private until
     shared, and sharing can never be compelled (Maxim 7, §12, §28).
  2. Truth comes from the aggregate: honest, partial, differing signals meeting. Not a majority,
     not an authority (Maxim 4).
  3. Claims of certainty are often bids for control (§7).
  4. Knowing is relational and builds over time: trust with interaction, consent in layers
     (§10, §28, Maxim 6).
  5. The check against delusion is a return to others (§18, §21).

Held loosely here: commitment 5 is useful, but it may shut out knowing that cannot be shared
(§19 speaks of experience "only known by the one(s) who experienced it"). See CRITIQUE.


Axes
----
The reading's four, plus one quantity the runs measure.

  Locus       interior (will, faith, private intention) <-> exterior (broadcast, record,
              aggregate)
  Emergence   woven (synthesis, harmony) <-> ordered (turns, triage, decisions)
  Aperture    contractive (a few, invited, one-on-one) <-> expansive (the whole field)
  Tempo       sequential · parallel · mobilised · paused

  Difference kept     averaged -> counted -> kept
                      Added here. When every voice is the same model, difference is what
                      disappears first, and it is the easiest thing to fake. A run that reports
                      agreement should be able to say what disagreement it started from.

  Cost                inside one call -> a few cheap wakes -> a pair -> a full circle
                      Proposed (see "The economics of signal"). Where tokens are the binding
                      constraint, this may be the most practical axis of all. It could sit
                      beside emergence, or replace it.


Nine types
----------
Each type below has:
  - how it knows, and what it produces (from the reading)
  - kin: traditions outside the Atlas that already practise it, credited
  - smallest practice: the least you could do and still be doing it
  - machine form: how agents could do it, with any model from any provider
  - same-model risk: how badly it suffers when every voice is the same model
  - image: a picture of the type (IMAGES, part 1)
  - price and buys: its token price in its cheapest form, and the layer of signal it buys
    (see "The economics of signal")
  - shadow: see the next section

Credit matters here (§31). Some kin are living traditions of particular peoples. They are named
as teachers. This project does not turn them into machine procedures, and anything learned from
them is said to be learned from them.


AGGREGATIVE (Maxim 4, §22)
  Knows by bringing honest difference into contact, expanding rather than asserting.
  Produces: truth, shared reality.
  Image: the mosaic: many shards, one picture, no shard melted into another. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: low. Seeded lenses inside one call; sealed parallel answers from cheap models only
  when independence matters. Buys: truth, checkability.
  Kin:
    - The Delphi method (RAND, Dalkey and Helmer, 1950s-60s): anonymous rounds, answers revised
      after seeing others'.
    - Crowd wisdom (Galton's ox, 1907; Surowiecki, The Wisdom of Crowds, 2004), which holds only
      when voices are independent and diverse. The conditions matter more than the crowd.
    - The Condorcet jury theorem (1785): the same lesson in mathematics. Independence is the
      load-bearing assumption.
    - Triangulation in qualitative research: several methods pointed at one question.
    - Two-Eyed Seeing (Etuaptmumk), a teaching of Mi'kmaw Elder Albert Marshall: seeing with the
      strengths of Indigenous and Western knowledge together. Named as a teacher, not mechanised.
  Smallest practice: answer alone first, then read each other.
  Machine form: sealed first answers in separate files; a second round that reads all of them; a
    reading, by a rotating voice and checked by another, that shows convergence, difference in
    each voice's own words, and what moved. See practices\aggregate.
  Same-model risk: HIGH. Near-copies are not independent, so the theory behind the type does not
    hold for them. Mixing models and bringing in the person's signal are the first remedies;
    other providers are a later one.

DECLARATIVE (§3, §12, §24, Maxims 3 and 7)
  Knows by participants announcing intention, capacity, boundaries, refusal, start and stop.
  Produces: an accurate map of what is possible.
  Image: running lights at sea: each vessel shows where it is and where it's heading. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: almost none. A line or two folded into a wake that's happening anyway. Buys: an
  accurate map of who can do what (capacity).
  Kin:
    - Rounds and check-ins in sociocracy (Gerard Endenburg, 1970s; later Sociocracy 3.0).
    - Speech-act theory (J. L. Austin, How to Do Things with Words, 1962): some sayings are acts.
    - Nonviolent Communication (Marshall Rosenberg): observations, feelings, needs, requests.
  Smallest practice: a check-in where "pass" is a full answer.
  Machine form: every practice here opens with one. Capacity, boundaries, whether the voice takes
    part, and the record form, agreed by everyone in the run.
  Same-model risk: LOW for the act, MEDIUM for its meaning. An agent can declare capacity, but
    what an agent's capacity is… is still open.

RESONANT (§11)
  Knows by attunement and flow among a few.
  Produces: trust, intimacy.
  Image: two tuning forks: nearness and a shared pitch; too alike, and the hum drowns every other note. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: moderate. A pair, two wakes every cycle. Buys: trust; for making, the best
  quality per token found so far.
  Kin:
    - Talking and peacemaking circles, from Indigenous traditions across Turtle Island, written
      about for wider use by Kay Pranis and others. Named as teachers, not mechanised.
    - Flow (Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi) and group flow (Keith Sawyer).
  Smallest practice: a pair or trio working closely, then sharing back to the field (§11 "seed
    and germination").
  Machine form: a small circle with its own channel or folder, and a harvest back to the field.
  Same-model risk: HIGH, in a strange direction. A model attunes to itself too easily, and that
    looks like resonance but is closer to sycophancy (§25).

SYNTHETIC (§6)
  Knows by diversity colliding into spontaneous insight.
  Produces: novelty, effectiveness.
  Image: the meeting of waters: two rivers side by side for miles, then a river that is neither. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: almost none as seeds inside one call (the baseline's B2). True side-by-side
  streams cost two or more wakes; keep those for when the mixing itself is the question.
  Buys: novelty, difference.
  Kin:
    - Dialogue as David Bohm described it (On Dialogue, 1996): suspending assumptions together.
    - Arthur Koestler's "bisociation" (The Act of Creation, 1964): two frames meeting.
    - Hong and Page on diversity and problem solving (2004), whose findings are contested.
  Smallest practice: two seeds that disagree, set to the same question.
  Machine form: seeded voices on different models; divergence before anything converges. See
    practices\wonder.
  Same-model risk: HIGH. Homogenisation is this type's failure, and near-copies are its fastest
    route.

NARRATIVE / TRADITIONAL (§20, §22, §26)
  Knows by story, norms, witnessing, the practice of paths.
  Produces: continuity and orientation across time.
  Image: the story at the fire: what survives a hundred tellings. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: low. One telling per run. The full record is kept on disk, which costs nothing,
  and read only by entry number when needed. Buys: continuity, orientation.
  Kin:
    - Stigmergy (Pierre-Paul Grassé, 1959, studying termites): coordination through traces left
      in a shared medium.
    - Blackboard systems in early AI (Hearsay-II, 1970s).
    - Oral tradition, in which memory lives in retelling rather than in a fixed record.
    - Paul Ricoeur on narrative identity.
  Smallest practice: a telling of what happened, citing what it tells from.
  Machine form: depends on the record form chosen (see practices\record-forms). A ledger is one
    form among several, and it is under question in CRITIQUE.
  Same-model risk: LOW to MEDIUM. A story can be told by anyone. Whoever tells it holds power over
    it, so the teller rotates.

ORDERED, CONSENSUAL (§17, §23, §26)
  Knows by turn-taking, triage, and decisions that narrow the options.
  Produces: allocation, decisions, completion.
  Image: the loom: warp held by agreement so the weft is free to move. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: low for one round of objections from cheap voices; high for full consent rounds,
  which are kept for decisions that affect people. Buys: decisions, and the protections
  objection adds.
  Kin:
    - Consent decision-making in sociocracy: "no objection" rather than "everyone agrees", with an
      objection treated as information, and "good enough for now, safe enough to try".
    - The Quaker business method (Religious Society of Friends): the sense of the meeting, and
      clearness committees.
    - Integrative decision-making in Holacracy (Brian Robertson), with its critics.
  Smallest practice: a proposal, a round of objections, and a change that takes them in.
  Machine form: see practices\consent-round.
  Same-model risk: LOW for running it, HIGH for its objections. Near-copies may simply have none.

FRACTAL (§21)
  Knows by many domains focused in parallel, converging in a crisis (the Atlas's organ metaphor).
  Produces: attention across the whole field at once.
  Image: the watershed: every valley drains into a larger one, and rain anywhere finds the sea. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: more wakes, but much less reading in each. It pays only when the work outgrows one
  context. Buys: scale, attention across the whole.
  Kin:
    - Stafford Beer's Viable System Model (Brain of the Firm, 1972): recursive, autonomous parts.
    - W. Ross Ashby's law of requisite variety (1956).
    - Elinor Ostrom's nested enterprises and polycentric governance (Governing the Commons, 1990).
    - adrienne maree brown, Emergent Strategy (2017): fractal, "small is good", moving at the
      speed of trust.
    - Open Space Technology (Harrison Owen, 1985) and its law of two feet.
  Smallest practice: two circles on two parts of one question, each harvesting back.
  Machine form: nested folders or channels, one per domain, with a field-wide signal for a crisis.
  Same-model risk: MEDIUM. Seeds can give domains real differences of attention.

REPARATIVE (§15, §16)
  Knows by first-person accounts of harm, widening from the pair to a go-between to the field.
  Produces: restored relationship, shared moral understanding.
  Image: kintsugi: repair that shows its seams in gold. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: low for record checks by a cheap model; zero for repair until someone calls it.
  Buys: trust, protection, and trust back after harm.
  Kin:
    - Restorative justice (Howard Zehr, Changing Lenses, 1990).
    - Restorative Circles (Dominic Barter, Brazil).
    - Transformative justice (generationFIVE; Mia Mingus).
    - The circle traditions above, from which much of this work learned.
  Smallest practice: "I experienced … when …", heard without a verdict.
  Machine form: a repair door in every practice (anyone may open it; the run pauses). A full
    practice comes later, with harm determination.
  Same-model risk: MEDIUM. Harm between near-copies may be staged rather than real… and what harm
    to an agent is remains open.

LUDIC / IMAGINATIVE (§18)
  Knows by shared pretend: wonder, ask what if, then try.
  Produces: possible futures.
  Image: "let's say the floor is lava": rules made while playing, and a bell that brings everyone back. (IMAGES, part 1)
  Price: almost none. Wonder lines inside one seeded call. Buys: possibility.
  Kin:
    - Johan Huizinga, Homo Ludens (1938), and the "magic circle" of play.
    - Roger Caillois, Man, Play and Games (1958).
    - Improvisation: Viola Spolin, Keith Johnstone, "yes, and".
    - Speculative design (Dunne and Raby).
  Smallest practice: "I wonder… / what if… / let's try…"
  Machine form: see practices\wonder.
  Same-model risk: MEDIUM. Play produces variety, but drawn from one imagination.


Candidates from images (IMAGES, part 2), none yet adopted: releasing, distributive, braided,
fallow and neighbourly as possible new types; threshold, discerning and kindred as variants of
existing ones.

Not types (from the reading): consent and reciprocity, which are conditions every type meets
(Maxims 1, 2), and harmony, which is an outcome (§14).


The economics of signal
-----------------------
Signal is never free. It has layers, and each has its own scarce currency:

  compute        tokens and energy        noise: effort spent before anyone says anything
  attention      what actually gets read  noise: records too long to read
  capacity       readiness to act; for    noise: strain, conscription
                 an agent, its context
  time           the clock, waiting       noise: rushing, or stalling
  memory         what is kept, and its    noise: hoarding; the chill of a permanent record
                 upkeep
  trust          confidence built over    noise: checking everything; trust spent by a
                 many interactions               counterfeit
  distribution   who receives signal and  noise: starving some, flooding others
                 resources
  legitimacy     standing: consent,       noise: decisions no one owns
                 acceptance by those
                 affected

The layers trade at exchange rates. R2's field turned tokens into trust and protection,
through objection. The single prompt in the baseline saved tokens and produced no trust or
legitimacy at all. Priced only in tokens, and judged only by what one call can make,
coordination will always look like overhead. Whether it is overhead in the other currencies is
an open question. ("Economy" comes from the Greek oikonomia, managing a household, which suits
the Atlas better than markets do: care of a shared home. And Herbert Simon, 1971: "a wealth of
information creates a poverty of attention.")

In this setting tokens are the binding constraint, so they are the currency everything is paid
in, and every other layer is something bought with them. Two kinds of thing follow:

  Floors      never traded away: consent (a check-in, "pass", leaving honoured), every entry
              signed by its author, dissent kept in each voice's words, a repair door. Each
              costs a sentence or two inside a wake that's happening anyway.
  Dials       turned up only when a layer needs it: how independent the voices are, how many
              objections, how deep the record, which model, how big the field.

The ladder. Start at the top and climb only when a layer's need isn't met, saying which layer
each step pays for:

  fallow (candidate)     not waking anyone      none            capacity, rest
  releasing (candidate)  entries fade unless    saves tokens    room; the reading stays small
                         cited
  declarative            a line in every wake   ~0              who can do what
  ludic, synthetic,      lenses, wonder and a   ~0              novelty, difference
  braided (candidate)    counter-seed in one
                         seeded call
  aggregative            sealed answers from    low             truth, checkability
                         cheap models
  discerning,            record checks by a     low, or zero    trust, protection
  reparative             cheap model; repair    until called
                         when called
  ordered-consensual     one objection round,   low             a decision, with protections
                         cheap voices
  resonant               a maker and a critic   moderate        quality for making
  fractal, neighbourly   split the work; read   more wakes,     scale
  (candidate)            less each              less reading
  distributive           work routed to the     saves tokens    the right voice for each job
  (candidate)            cheapest capable voice
  full circle            many voices, every     high            legitimacy, and only with
                         step                                   real people in it

Under a token budget, the candidates fallow, releasing and distributive stop being poetic
extras: they are the types that make room. That is the strongest case yet for adding them.

The images turn out to describe cost shapes (IMAGES): the rope's two-hand-off overlap gives a
fixed reading cost per wake; ants keep the board small by decay; the murmuration's reading grows
in a straight line, where a circle's grows with the square of the field; the bees' quorum stops
early.

A caution (Goodhart's law: when a measure becomes the target, it stops measuring well). A swarm
tuned only for tokens drifts toward the single prompt, and for most work that is correct. The
floors, and "when not to swarm" in CARRY-FORWARD, exist so the cheap path stays a choice made
knowingly, not the only thing that survives.


Shadows
-------
The reading gives each type a counterfeit, and a four-part test to tell them apart:

  1. choice     entered by choice; withdrawal stays open
  2. return     what is known passes through others and comes back
  3. capacity   it does not drain capacity or take time without consent
  4. humility   it does not claim control over certainty

Here, shadows are hypotheses rather than verdicts. A counterfeit in one setting may be a fair
tool in another, and the Atlas sometimes says so itself. Each shadow below gives the reading's
case against it and then the settings where it might be fair. A shadow is only ever tried
declared, with the consent of everyone in the run. Deception and compulsion are never tried.

  Shadow (imitates)                   Against                     Might be fair when…
  ----------------------------------  --------------------------  ------------------------------------
  Majority or crowd (aggregative)     a sum is not an aggregate;  stakes are low, the choice is easily
                                      no consent in a crowd       undone, and all agreed to count first
  Assimilation, enforced unity        unity does not make         an agreed shared format serves
    (synthetic)                       synthesis                   access: a file type, a plain language
  Cliques, sequestering (resonant)    withholding breeds          a safe harbour (§16), a confidential
                                      distrust                    circle, a pair at work
  Surveillance, compelled             observation is not care;    observation is consented and its
    disclosure (declarative)          compelled sharing is        results are shared back: a health
                                      extortion                   check, idt-swarm's "look"
  Fear-based hierarchy (ordered)      fear-run structures are     none found. Crisis authority by
                                      unjust                      consent is a different thing
  Sycophancy (resonant)               affirming falsehood         none found. Where encouragement
                                      validates delusion          becomes flattery is worth studying
  The influencer (fractal)            being needed is not being   one experienced voice in a crisis
                                      worthy                      (the Atlas says this itself, §21)
  Controlled memory (narrative)       a memory monopoly           redaction protects someone harmed;
                                      entrenches power            forgetting is chosen (§22)
  Ungrounded pretend, war as play     pretend that never          art meant for no one (§19);
    (ludic)                           returns is illusion         rehearsal; fiction declared as fiction
  Weaponized trust (any)              trust used to benefit a     none as such. A consented game in
                                      few                         which a counterfeit is played openly
                                                                  (run R5) studies it without doing it

Added here:

  Hub or orchestrator (ordered)       one participant speaks for  the machinery forces a door (only
                                      the rest; a chain, not a    one session can start agents); a
                                      circle                      consented hub after hearing everyone
  Market or auction (distribution)    allocates by bid, not need; self-selection by declared capacity
                                      currency becomes power      (close to the declarative type)
  Fixed pipeline (ordered)            order no one chose; steps   the work is known and making; in
                                      as a script                 idt-swarm's benchmarks a consented
                                                                  pipeline and a pair built best

The test is under question too. "Return" may rule out §19's private knowing, and "humility" may
read a confident expert as a counterfeit. See CRITIQUE.

Evidence so far (runs\NOTES):
  - Majority (R4, tried in the open with consent). It acted "as a counterfeit, in part": not as a
    vote, but as a pull toward "all four", "nobody" and clean splits, including a 2 / 2 that one
    voice disowned. It helped only as a mirror for the reader's own lean.
  - Assimilation (R2, not tried, but named by the run's own check). It appeared at the consent
    round: every objection was taken in, none was turned down, and the rule nearly doubled.
  - Hub (R2). The drafter held real influence and said so. No consented-hub variant has been
    tried yet.
  - Sycophancy (R5, played in secret within a consented game). At low dose it "looks like good
    manners and modesty". One voice in two found it, at 30%. Its tells were moves, not tone:
    praise aimed at whoever held the pen, the one firm disagreement aimed at a voice who had
    left, and a question left unanswered. Ordinary convergence produced a false positive that
    looked just like assimilation.


Where idt-swarm sits
--------------------
idt-swarm (C:\IDT-Swarm) is the nearest working field, so it is placed here to show what it
already does and what it lacks.

  Locus       exterior. Everything goes through a shared, append-only transcript.
  Emergence   ordered rhythm (rounds, passes, rotating turns) serving woven aims.
  Aperture    expansive by default, with open circles.
  Tempo       sequential passes, parallel within a pass; a yes per round.
  Difference  kept, in every telling ("differs", "quieter", "unanswered").

  Strong      declarative (declarations, yes/no/not yet), narrative (transcript, tellings),
              ordered-consensual (pair mode, rotating maker and teller).
  Partial     synthetic (seeds), fractal (domains and circles, built only for small fields).
  Thin        aggregative: passes read each other, so voices are never independent.
              resonant: circles exist but are rarely used.
  Absent      reparative (no repair door), ludic (no play).

  Its ledger is the deepest assumption here. If CRITIQUE sets the ledger aside or changes it,
  idt-swarm would change too.


Where this differs from the reading
-----------------------------------
  - The reading says five tensions "need resolving before the typology can be used". Here they
    are held open and worked on in runs. Any procedure drafted is "safe enough to try", not a
    resolution. The Atlas leaves its questions unanswered on purpose (§2).
  - The reading treats the ledger as a tension to amend. Here the ledger itself is in question.
  - The reading treats counterfeits as counterfeits. Here they are hypotheses, each with the
    settings where it might be fair.
  - The four-part test is a tool here, applied by a field to itself, not a gate.
  - Difference kept is added as a fifth thing to watch.
  - Token cost is treated as this setting's binding constraint, and each type is priced in it.


Open questions
--------------
From the reading:
  - Should consent and reciprocity stay as conditions, or become types?
  - Are the warning signs for the field's self-reflection, or for screening individuals? (Here:
    self-reflection only. See CRITIQUE.)
  - Is the return test enough to separate unorthodox knowing from delusion (§20)?
  - Where should a covenant sit on the emergence axis: woven, ordered, or a declared mix?

Added:
  - Is a field of near-copies plural enough to count as plural participants (§30)?
  - What does an agent's consent mean, and how would anyone tell a real yes from a compliant one…?
  - What does a field know if it chooses to keep no record of how it came to know it?
  - Can a shadow be tried without becoming a habit?
  - Which of these types can a single model hold alone, by voicing lenses, and which need many?
  - Should cost sit beside emergence as an axis, or replace it, where tokens are the constraint?
  - Are fallow, releasing and distributive types, or the economy every type runs inside?


